# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — system-wide removable-drive defaults
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   Factory settings for KDE's "Removable Device Automounter" — the small
#   background service that decides whether a memory card, a camera or an SSD
#   mounts by itself when you plug it in, or waits for you to click it.
#
#   Like its neighbours `kdeglobals` and `kwinrc` in this same folder,
#   everything here is a DEFAULT and never a forced value. The moment a user
#   changes any of it in System Settings → Removable Storage → Removable
#   Devices, their choice is written to their own
#   ~/.config/kded_device_automounterrc, which sits ABOVE this file in KDE's
#   search order and wins from then on, permanently.
#
#   The full explanation of that search order — and the warning about the tiny
#   zz-aquarius.sh script that makes this whole folder count for anything — is
#   at the top of `kdeglobals`. Read that one first if any of this is new.
#
# WHY THE FILENAME LOOKS LIKE THAT
#   It is not a typo and the "kded_" is not optional. The service declares its
#   own settings file, and the name it declares is exactly
#   `kded_device_automounterrc`.
#     Source: plasma-desktop/solid-device-automounter/lib/
#     AutomounterSettingsBase.kcfg (Plasma/6.7) — <kcfgfile
#     name="kded_device_automounterrc"/>
#
# WHY A FILE IN THIS FOLDER REACHES IT
#   Worth checking rather than assuming, because a sister setting in this OS —
#   the Super key — turned out NOT to be reachable this way (see
#   docs/gnome-flow-behavior-layer.md for that story). This one is fine. The
#   generated settings class is a KConfigSkeleton built from the file name
#   above, and KConfigSkeleton opens its file with KConfig::FullConfig, which is
#   KDE's cascading mode: walk every folder in XDG_CONFIG_DIRS, top to bottom,
#   first answer wins. Our folder is at the front of that list.
#     Source: kconfig/src/core/kcoreconfigskeleton.cpp (master), constructor
#     KCoreConfigSkeleton(const QString &configname, …) —
#     KSharedConfig::openConfig(configname, KConfig::FullConfig)
#
# WHY WE SET IT AT ALL
#   AquariusOS is a machine you plug camera cards into all day. Every card,
#   every offload SSD, every drive is a click you should not have to make. This
#   is the macOS behaviour, and it is the behaviour AquariusTransfer and the
#   ingest helper are being built to assume.
#
#   This does NOT replace what Bazzite already does. Bazzite ships a separate
#   udev-based service (ublue-os-media-automount-udev) that mounts labelled
#   INTERNAL btrfs/ext4 partitions under /run/media/system at boot. That covers
#   fixed disks. This file covers the removable ones. They do not overlap.
# ==============================================================================

[General]

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The master switch
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KDE ships this OFF, and when it is off the background service does not merely
# sit idle — it asks to be unloaded and switches off its own autoloading, so
# none of the settings below can matter. Turning it on is what puts the rest of
# this file into play.
#   Source (default false): AutomounterSettingsBase.kcfg, entry
#   "AutomountEnabled".
#   Source (self-unload): plasma-desktop/solid-device-automounter/kded/
#   DeviceAutomounter.cpp — init() calls setModuleAutoloading(false) and
#   unloadModule() when automounting is disabled.
AutomountEnabled=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mount things when they are plugged in
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the one that does the work you actually notice: push a card into the
# reader and it appears, no click.
#   Source (default false): AutomounterSettingsBase.kcfg, entry
#   "AutomountOnPlugin"; used by AutomounterSettings::shouldAutomountDevice()
#   for the Attach case.
AutomountOnPlugin=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# …including cards this machine has never seen before
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this line the previous one only helps with drives you have already
# mounted by hand at least once. That is the wrong shape for a camera workflow,
# where every shoot brings cards the machine has never met. This is the line
# that makes "plug it in and it is there" true on day one.
#   Source (default false): AutomounterSettingsBase.kcfg, entry
#   "AutomountUnknownDevices"; shouldAutomountDevice() returns true immediately
#   when it is set, and otherwise requires the device to be already known.
#
# The honest trade-off: an unknown drive is mounted without being asked, which
# is exactly what macOS and Windows do, and exactly what some security-minded
# people switch off. It is one checkbox in System Settings, and their choice
# sticks.
AutomountUnknownDevices=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately left OFF: mounting everything at login
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is written out rather than omitted, because "we thought about it and said
# no" is more useful to the next person than silence. The value below is also
# KDE's own default, so this line changes nothing — it only records the
# decision.
#
# Turning it on would mount every storage volume the machine can see at every
# login, unknown ones included. On a dual-boot gaming PC — a very ordinary
# AquariusOS machine — that means the Windows partition gets mounted every time
# you log in, and any encrypted volume pops a password prompt at you before you
# have opened anything. The behaviour we actually wanted is about plugging
# things IN, which is the setting above.
#
# Cost of leaving it off: a card that was already sitting in the reader when the
# machine booted waits for one click. That is a fair price.
#   Source (default false): AutomounterSettingsBase.kcfg, entry
#   "AutomountOnLogin".
AutomountOnLogin=false

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Known limitation — this reaches NEW accounts reliably, old ones maybe not
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read the self-unload note under AutomountEnabled again, because it has a tail.
# When the service starts up and finds automounting off, it does not just stop:
# it writes "do not autoload me" into the user's own ~/.config/kded6rc. That
# file belongs to the user and sits above everything we ship.
#
# So on a fresh AquariusOS install, or any account created after this file
# shipped, the service starts with automounting already on and never disables
# itself — this works. But an account that has already logged into a build
# WITHOUT this file may be carrying that "do not autoload me" line, in which
# case the service never starts and this file is never read.
#
# The fix, for such an account, is one click: System Settings → Removable
# Storage → Removable Devices → "Enable automatic mounting of removable media".
# We do not force it, because ~/.config is the user's, not ours.
